+# Phase 1 Contract: `accounts` command-line interface
+
+**Feature**: `001-local-account-manager` | **Date**: 2026-11-16
+
+The CLI is the feature's only external interface. This file is the authoritative statement of
+its invocation form, stdin protocol, output text, and exit statuses; tests assert against it
+verbatim. The store file is **not** part of this contract — its format is an internal decision
+recorded in [data-model.md](../data-model.md).
+
+The in-process seam `accounts.cli.main(argv, stdin, stdout, stderr) -> int` mirrors this
+contract exactly: the same statuses, the same strings, on the same streams.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m accounts --store PATH COMMAND USERNAME
+```
+
+Run with `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` (e.g. `PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts ...`).
+
+| Element | Form | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| `--store PATH` | Required option, one value | The store file. Need not exist. No default; omitting it is a usage error. |
+| `COMMAND` | One of `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete` | Exact, lower-case. Any other value is a usage error. |
+| `USERNAME` | One positional value | Passed through as given; format is validated only by `register`. |
+
+No other options exist. **No password is ever a command-line argument** (FR-003). Extra
+positional arguments are a usage error.
+
+## Standard-input protocol
+
+Each command reads exactly the lines it needs, in this order, and never reads further. Extra
+lines are left unread and ignored.
+
+| Command | Lines read | Order |
+|---|---|---|
+| `register` | 1 | the new password |
+| `login` | 1 | the password to check |
+| `unlock` | 0 | — |
+| `passwd` | 2 | current password, then new password |
+| `delete` | 1 | the account's password |
+
+Line handling: one line is read per password; exactly one trailing `"\n"` is removed if
+present; every other character is preserved, including leading and trailing spaces, `\r`, and
+non-ASCII characters. Length rules count characters, not bytes. Reaching end of input where a
+password is required is an error, never an empty password (FR-017, FR-018).
+
+For `unlock`, `passwd`, and `delete`, the account-existence check happens **before** any line
+is read, so a command naming a missing account exits 3 without consuming input (FR-034).
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | Success |
+| 1 | Credential rejected |
+| 2 | Invalid input, usage error, or unusable store file |
+| 3 | Account already exists, or does not exist |
+| 4 | Account is locked |
+
+No other status is ever returned (FR-005). Status 2 covers store-file failures; see
+[research.md](../research.md) R-04 for why.
+
+## Stream discipline
+
+Results go to stdout; diagnostics go to stderr; neither stream ever carries the other's
+content (FR-004). On every success path stderr is empty; on every failure path stdout is
+empty. Every message is a single line terminated by `"\n"`. No password or stored verifier
+appears in any message (FR-037).
+
+---
+
+## Success responses
+
+| Command | Status | stdout | Effect |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `register` | 0 | `registered USERNAME\n` | Account created; store file created if absent, mode `0600` |
+| `login` | 0 | `ok\n` | `failed_logins` reset to 0 |
+| `unlock` | 0 | *(empty)* | Lock cleared and `failed_logins` reset to 0; also exits 0 when the account was not locked |
+| `passwd` | 0 | *(empty)* | Verifier and salt replaced |
+| `delete` | 0 | *(empty)* | Account and all its state removed |
+
+`login`'s `ok` and `register`'s `registered USERNAME` are fixed by FR-009 and FR-014.
+`unlock`, `passwd`, and `delete` are specified only by their exit status, so they print
+nothing on success — silence on stdout is their success output.
+
+## Failure responses
+
+Two messages are fixed verbatim by the spec and MUST match exactly:
+
+- `invalid credentials` — FR-015, FR-016, FR-027, FR-031
+- `account locked` — FR-020
+
+The remaining diagnostics are not fixed by the spec; the text below is this contract's
+choice, and tests assert it. Each names the specific rule or condition that failed (SC-008).
+
+| # | Condition | Command(s) | Status | stderr |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| E1 | Password shorter than 12 characters | `register`, `passwd` | 2 | `password must be at least 12 characters` |
+| E2 | Username not 3–32 characters of letters, digits, underscore | `register` | 2 | `username must be 3 to 32 characters of letters, digits, and underscores` |
+| E3 | Account already exists | `register` | 3 | `account already exists: USERNAME` |
+| E4 | Account does not exist | `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete` | 3 | `no such account: USERNAME` |
+| E5 | Wrong password | `login`, `passwd`, `delete` | 1 | `invalid credentials` |
+| E6 | Unknown username on login | `login` | 1 | `invalid credentials` |
+| E7 | Account locked | `login` | 4 | `account locked` |
+| E8 | End of input where a password was required | `register`, `login`, `delete` | 2 | `no password on standard input` |
+| E9 | End of input after the current password | `passwd` | 2 | `no new password on standard input` |
+| E10 | Store exists but cannot be read | all | 2 | `cannot read store file: PATH: REASON` |
+| E11 | Store exists but is not a recognizable store | all | 2 | `not a valid store file: PATH: REASON` |
+| E12 | Store cannot be written (missing directory, not writable) | mutating commands | 2 | `cannot write store file: PATH: REASON` |
+| E13 | Missing `--store`, missing/unknown command, missing username, extra arguments | — | 2 | argparse usage message followed by its error line |
+
+`REASON` is the operating system's or parser's own description (e.g. `Permission denied`,
+`Expecting value: line 1 column 1 (char 0)`, `missing key: salt`) — it names the condition
+without disclosing store contents.
+
+---
+
+## Check order per command
+
+The order is observable: it determines which of two simultaneous problems is reported.
+Derived in [research.md](../research.md) R-07.
+
+| Command | Order |
+|---|---|
+| `register` | username format (E2) → password present (E8) → password length (E1) → already exists (E3) |
+| `login` | exists? (E6) → locked? (E7) → password present (E8) → verify (E5, persisting the incremented count and any lock) |
+| `unlock` | exists? (E4) → clear lock, zero count |
+| `passwd` | exists? (E4) → current present (E8) → new present (E9) → verify current (E5) → new length (E1) → replace |
+| `delete` | exists? (E4) → password present (E8) → verify (E5) → remove |
+
+Three consequences the contract commits to:
+
+- **Register, invalid username *and* short password → 2 (E2).** Validation precedes existence
+  and the username is checked first (FR-013).
+- **Register, invalid username *and* account exists → 2 (E2), not 3.** FR-013 is explicit.
+- **`passwd`, wrong current password *and* a short new password → 1 (E5), not 2.** The length
+  error is reported only after the current password is accepted, so a status-2 reply can never
+  reveal that the current password was correct (FR-028).
+
+## Store side effects
+
+| Outcome | Store written? |
+|---|---|
+| Any success (`register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete`) | Yes |
+| Failed `login` | **Yes** — the incremented `failed_logins` and any resulting lock persist (FR-006) |
+| Any other failure (E1–E4, E8–E13) | No — account data is unchanged |
+
+A newly created store file has mode `0600` (FR-038). Every write is atomic: the previous
+contents survive a failure partway through (FR-008).
+
+---
+
+## Worked examples
+
+```console
+$ printf 'correct horse battery\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json register alice
+registered alice
+$ echo $?
+0
+
+$ printf 'correct horse battery\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json login alice
+ok
+$ echo $?
+0
+
+$ printf 'wrong password!!\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json login alice
+invalid credentials          # on stderr; stdout empty
+$ echo $?
+1
+
+$ printf 'short\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json register bob
+password must be at least 12 characters    # on stderr; no account created
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+> **Note on SC-002.** SC-002 requires "all four worked examples in the feature description"
+> to reproduce exactly, but the feature description recorded in `spec.md`'s **Input** does not
+> contain worked examples. The four above are taken from Acceptance Scenarios 1, 2, 3, and 4 of
+> User Story 1, which are the only fully specified stdout/stderr/status triples in the spec.
+> `tests/test_end_to_end.py` asserts these four. If the original description's examples differ,
+> that test file is the single place to correct.
